[b]The XORG Directive[/b]
The XORG (Extended ORG) directive is designed to facilitate "interleaved assembly," where you need to generate code for multiple different memory areas within a single source file without manually managing the program counter for each one.

It functions similarly to the standard ORG directive but adds the ability to "link" a symbol to the program counter P%.

[b]Syntax[/b]
XORG <expression>
XORG <symbol>

[b]How it works[/b]
Static Origin (XORG <expression>)
If you provide a numeric expression (e.g., XORG &2000 or XORG start + 10), XORG behaves exactly like the standard ORG directive. It stops tracking any previous symbol and sets the current assembly address P% to the value of the specified symbol.

Tracking Origin (XORG <symbol>)
If you provide a single symbol name (e.g., XORG main_code), XORG performs four steps:

It unlinks any symbol that is currently linked.
It sets the assembly address P% to the current value of that supplied symbol.
It links the supplied symbol to P%.
As subsequent code is assembled and P% advances, the value of the linked symbol is automatically updated to match.
This allows you to define "cursors" for different memory sections. You can assemble a block of code for one section, switch to another, and then switch back to the first; XORG will ensure you resume exactly where you left off.

ORG functionality has been unchanged except that it also unlinks any symbol attached to P%.

Example of using XORG
[code]
;---------------------------
; Define memory map base addresses
;---------------------------

main = &1000			; Start address for Main RAM code
sideways = &8000		; Start address for Sideways RAM code
zp = &0070			; Start address for Zero Page variables

;---------------------------
; Initialize context cursors
;---------------------------

prog = main			; 'prog' tracks the PC for Main RAM
util = sideways			; 'util' tracks the PC for Sideways RAM
vars = zp			; 'vars' tracks the PC for Zero Page variables

;---------------------------
; Main RAM Context
;---------------------------

	xorg prog		; Set P% = prog. 'prog' is now linked to P% and will auto-update.

.start				; Begin assembling code in Main RAM

	jsr delay1		; Call routine located in Sideways RAM context

	jsr delay2		; Call routine located in Main RAM context

	jmp start

;---------------------------
; Sideways RAM Context
;---------------------------

	xorg util		; Switch to Sideways RAM.
					; 'prog' is unlinked (retains current value).
					; P% = util. 'util' is now linked to P%.

.delay1				; Begin assembling code in Sideways RAM

	lda #0
	sta counter1 + 0
	sta counter1 + 1

.delay1loop

	dec counter1 + 0
	bne delay1loop
	dec counter1 + 1
	bne delay1loop

	rts

;---------------------------
; Zero Page Context
;---------------------------

	xorg vars		; Switch to Zero Page.
					; 'util' is unlinked.
					; P% = vars. 'vars' is now linked to P%.

.counter1			; Define label at current ZP address (&0070)

	skip 2			; Reserve 2 bytes. 'vars' advances to &0072.

;---------------------------
; Resume Main RAM Context
;---------------------------

	xorg prog		; Switch back to Main RAM.
					; 'vars' is unlinked.
					; P% = prog (restores previous Main RAM PC). 'prog' re-linked.

.delay2				; Assemble more code in Main RAM, continuing from where we left off.

	lda #0
	sta counter2 + 0
	sta counter2 + 1

.delay2loop

	dec counter2 + 0
	bne delay2loop
	dec counter2 + 1
	bne delay2loop

	rts

	org *			; Standard ORG directive.
					; This unlinks the currently active symbol ('prog').
					; P% remains unchanged, but 'prog' stops tracking it.

	equw 0			; Emit 2 bytes. P% advances by 2.

;---------------------------
; Resume Zero Page Context
;---------------------------

	xorg vars		; Switch back to Zero Page.
					; P% = vars (restores &0072). 'vars' re-linked.

.counter2			; Define label at &0072.

	skip 2			; Reserve 2 bytes. 'vars' advances to &0074.

;---------------------------
[/code]

