[b]Part 3: Volatile Symbol Assignment (@)[/b]
I was in the flow, so I decided to add the ability to use symbols as variables via a special modifier.

The underlying system that allows XORG to link symbols to the Program Counter and update them across multiple passes is already in place. It was a natural progression to extend this functionality, allowing any symbol to act as a mutable variable rather than a fixed constant.

[b]How it Works[/b]
By default, symbols in the assembler are treated as constants once the code stabilizes. Appending the @ modifier to a symbol name (e.g., my_symbol@ = ...) marks it as volatile.

[b]Syntax & Example[/b]
The @ must immediately follow the symbol name with no intervening spaces.
    counter@ = counter + 1

[b]When a symbol is marked volatile:[/b]
    Continuous Updates: The assembler will update the symbol's value during every pass of the assembly process.
    Stability Bypass: The assembler will ignore changes to this specific symbol when deciding if the program has "stabilized." This prevents the assembler from entering an infinite loop if a symbol’s value fluctuates between passes.
 
Quick example
[code]
	org &1000
count = 0
	lda #count
count@ = count+1
	lda #count
count@ = count+1
	lda #count
count@ = count+1
	lda #count
[/code]

